Sources for splicing:
- Choquet, Patop & Churchman (2025) - The regulation and function of post-transcriptional RNA splicing, Nature Reviews Genetics - https://www.nature.com/articles/s41576-025-00836-z? - It explicitly describes splicing as a layer of gene-expression regulation, including regulation of protein production through transcript processing, nuclear retention and degradation.
- RNA splicing - a central layer of gene regulation (2025), Nature Reviews Genetics - https://www.nature.com/articles/s41576-025-00846-x? - It states that alternative splicing expands transcriptomic and proteomic diversity and emphasizes splicing as a central regulatory process
- Kornblihtt et al. (2013) - Alternative splicing: a pivotal step between eukaryotic transcription and translation, Nature Reviews Molecular Cell Biology - https://www.nature.com/articles/nrm3525? - splicing connects transcriptional regulation to downstream RNA/protein function. It discusses transcription–splicing coupling and how transcription can influence alternative splicing decisions.
- Rogalska, Vivori & Valcárcel (2023) - Regulation of pre-mRNA splicing: roles in physiology and disease, and therapeutic prospects, Nature Reviews Genetics - https://www.nature.com/articles/s41576-022-00556-8 - Strong modern general review covering the regulation of splicing machinery, physiological isoforms and pathological splicing alterations
- Ule & Blencowe (2019) - Alternative Splicing Regulatory Networks: Functions, Mechanisms, and Evolution, Molecular Cell - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31626751/ - introduce the idea of splicing programs/networks. It describes coordinated alternative-splicing events across physiological and disease states and the regulatory networks controlling them
- Fu & Ares (2014) - Context-dependent control of alternative splicing by RNA-binding proteins, Nature Reviews Genetics - https://www.nature.com/articles/nrg3778? - the same RNA-binding proteins can produce different splicing outcomes depending on their position and cellular context. This supports the idea that splicing contributes to cell-state-specific regulation

Sources for 3' scRNA splicing limitations:
-Arzalluz-Luque & Conesa, 2018 "Single-cell RNAseq for the study of isoforms—how is that possible?" https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6085759/ - partial sequencing naturally misses some events and can make different isoforms impossible to distinguish
- Westoby et al., 2020 "Obstacles to detecting isoforms using full-length scRNA-seq data" https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s13059-020-01981-w? - even with full-length scRNA-seq, high dropout rates are a major obstacle to alternative-splicing analysis
- Chen et al., 2020 "Technological advances and computational approaches for alternative splicing analysis in single cells" https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7033300/ 
- Joglekar et al. / SCATS (2020) - “Detecting differential alternative splicing events in scRNA-seq with or without Unique Molecular Identifiers.”  https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7299405/ - prevents from making the overly strong claim that scRNA-seq cannot detect splicing at all
